title green alone, red in company -- the flake had a mechanism and it was seven pens in my own lane
prompt the standing baton -- take the next agent-doable lap, Lindy-first crux-first, reds first
rota lap 4145, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. The threshold's hard rule reads "a guard that cannot red guards nothing -- a witness must be proven able to make a sound before its silence means anything." Its other face is what this lap repaired: a guard that reds when nothing is wrong has a sound that means nothing either, and the fleet learns to disbelieve it. The row asks what the work is FOR, and a roster pass is for being believed.
think The card carried my own open question -- a flake fired twice more, one of them amphora_carry_negative, in my lane -- and named a hypothesis, "both are controls that fork." I went looking for a mechanism rather than accepting the hypothesis.
think The first read answered it. tools/am/amphora_carry_negative_witness.rish opened with let vessel = "/tmp/amphora_cneg_vessel.bron" and let dock = "/tmp/amphora_cneg_dock" -- constants. Eight ships run one roster from eight checkouts on one pier with TMPDIR unset, so a constant pen is one directory on all eight.
think Seven of the family's eight witnesses carried it; the eighth, amphora_restore_negative, had taken mktemp -d when REDS %512 booked the class, and nothing else in the family had followed. The shape was one file away and nobody had walked it.
think Rather than repair on the reading, I proved the race first, because a repair whose fault was never demonstrated is a guess that happens to be green afterwards.
obs THE RACE IS PROVEN IN BOTH DIRECTIONS ON METAL. Four concurrent copies of the elder file read TWO RED and TWO GREEN, and the two reds failed at DIFFERENT assertions -- line 55 "empty names torn" and line 77 "final heal must pass". One fault gives one assertion; a race gives whichever one the peer happened to reach. Four concurrent copies of the repaired file read four green, and sixteen concurrent runs across the whole repaired family read zero red.
obs AND THE GUARD'S OWN "NOT PROVEN" IS ANSWERED FOR THIS PIER. shared_pen_scan.sh states its limit plainly: that any pen is ever ACTUALLY contended depends on which ships run which guard at which second. Read from /proc by each process's own working directory: all eight ships had a roster pass live at one instant, starts spread over 164 seconds -- 05:55:10 Petrichor to 05:57:54 Pheromone -- against a pass that ran 1,422 seconds here. The passes overlap for nearly their whole length. Contention is the expected state of every lap on this pier rather than a coincidence, which is why each firing looked like a fresh mystery.
obs THE REPAIR IS A RATCHET AND IT MOVED THE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST. constant_pen_files 61 -> 54, sites 308 -> 265, and wiping_files 7 -> 4. The wiping count is the one that guard's header says to drive to zero, because a file that merely writes a shared pen may interleave and survive while one that rm -rf's it takes a peer's in-flight state down. This lane held three of the seven. Both ceilings lowered to what the tree now reads; the witness is GREEN at 27 behaviors, 0 faults, against the new numbers.
obs THE REPAIR MEASURED A DEFECT IN THE INSTRUMENT THAT MEASURED IT. The scan reads wipe-or-hold off the whole line its /tmp token stands on, and the commonest shape in this tree declares the pen on one line and wipes the VARIABLE on another. Six of my seven wipe; the scan read three. Crossing every hold file against a plain grep for rm -r named six candidates tree-wide, and reading closed two as genuine wipers hidden from the count -- ios_app_shell_witness and macos_app_bundle_witness, both rostered -- one as a true hold, and three as mine. The honest population is 6 and the scan reads 4. Booked by stamp 20260907.062649.
obs THE HEADER HAD ALREADY NAMED THAT BLIND SPOT AND DECLINED IT, AND THE PREMISE IS WHAT FAILED. It reads "a variable tracker for one live instance is a cost with nothing to buy." There were six, half of them in one lane, and they were invisible for exactly the reason the decision cited as making them rare. A limitation written down honestly still hides its own size, because the sentence naming a blind spot is written from inside it.
obs A SECOND FLAKE MECHANISM SURFACED IN THE SAME PASS AND IT IS NOT A PEN. stash_record read red at 5s inside the pass and green standing alone twice on the same tree, and it names no /tmp path at all. The runner announced evidence construction/standing-equipment-reds/stash_record.txt on the line above its verdict and never wrote it -- the red room holds a file for each of the other two reds and all three gates, five of five, and nothing for this one. Three verdict lines in the same pass carried more than one guard name fused together, so five guards have no verdict line of their own. A fused line means a verdict can be lost, and a lost verdict is indistinguishable from a guard that never ran. Reported to the runner's lane rather than taken.
obs THE COLD OPEN'S OTHER RED WAS MINE AND CLOSED FIRST. index_row_bound read rows_duplicate=1 and rows_misordered=1 -- my own 20260907.023053 row standing twice on today's shelf after a rebase auto-merged it, the %440 shape. Deduped and sorted newest-first; 20 rows to 19, verdict ok.
obs THE LEDGER PIN WAS FULL AND I FOLDED A PEER'S BOOKED ROW RATHER THAN SHAVE THREE FIELDS. REDS.md stood at 39,260 of 40,960 and an honest row costs more than the 1,700 free. %535 was the pin's only BOOKED row -- instances repaired, remainder a rising adoption floor -- and the law says a BOOKED row may fold while an OPEN one stays. Folded whole, nothing closed or edited, its stamp unmoved, and the shelf header says plainly why a peer's row and not my own. The card was likewise at 40,813 of 40,960 with 147 free, so my account went to a shelf and the card kept a pointer; it closes at 40,956, four bytes under.
